.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sketch
is Ivan Sutherland's Sketchpad (1963) for a
.IR rio (1)
window.
A drawing is points, lines, arcs and text, held together by constraints:
lines that meet share a point, and constraints such as horizontal, parallel or equal length
hold among the points.
.I Sketch
moves the drawing until its constraints hold.
.PP
The
.I file
is a
.IR pic (1)
picture, with the constraints in comments that begin
.BR "# sk" ,
so
.B "pic file | troff | page"
prints it.
If
.I file
doesn't exist, the drawing starts empty, and
.B write
makes it.
.PP
With
.BR -s ,
.I sketch
draws nothing: it reads a drawing from
.I file
or standard input, satisfies its constraints, and writes it to standard output.
It names each constraint it could not satisfy on standard error, and exits with status
.BR unmet .
.PP
With
.BR -l ,
.I sketch
writes to
.I log
what the mouse and keys did, as atoms (see Atoms).
With
.BR -d ,
it opens no window: it reads atoms from standard input and does what they say,
as if the mouse and keys had,
writing
.I file
only when they type
.BR w ;
it puts what it would show on the message line on standard error, and exits at the end of its input.
So
.B "sketch -d file <log"
does again what the session that wrote
.I log
did, from the same
.IR file .
.SS Pointing
A red mark follows the mouse and shows what it is at:
a ring at a point, a dot on a line or arc, a cross where two cross, a square at a text,
and a small dot at nothing.
That mark, not the mouse, is where a new point goes.
Commands act on what it is at.
It takes a point within 5/64 inch, and a line, an arc or a crossing within 5/32.
.PP
While something is drawn or moved, faint guides show:
lines at the drawing's slopes, level and upright unless
.B /
changes them, through the points the moving ones are joined to,
and those points' lines extended,
and the same through the designated points, the anchor, and the hot points
.RB ( e ),
with circles around them of the drawing's lengths, which
.B o
chooses; and, while a point is placed, the middles of the lines that stay put.
The anchor's lines are at their slopes from the line or arc it ends, if it ends one,
so its level line there is the tangent and its upright the normal.
The mark takes a guide, or where it crosses a line or another guide,
and the message line says which, tersely:
.B "∠60° P1"
is the 60° line through
.BR P1 ,
.B "∥L1 P2"
and
.B "⊥L1 P2"
the anchor's tangent and normal on
.BR L1 ,
.B ∈L3
.B L3
extended,
.B ½L1
its middle,
.B "○P1 S1"
and
.B "○P1 |L3|"
circles about
.B P1
of
.BR S1
or
.BR L3 's
length, and
.B ∩
where two meet.
Letting go on a guide puts the point there and makes no constraint,
as Gargoyle's snapping does.
During a drag,
.B g
turns this gravity off or on, and
.B <
and
.B >
halve or double its reach.
.SS Mouse
.TF button\ 3
.TP
button 1
Draw a line from the mark, using a point there if there is one; its end follows the mouse.
Letting go on a point joins them; on a line or arc, the end goes on it.
A click with no drag leaves a point.
Just after
.BR c ,
draw an arc around the anchor instead.
Letting go, the message line gives the length drawn and its angle,
.BR "L2 = 1 ∠120°" ,
or the arc's radius and sweep.
.TP
button 2
Move what is under the mouse: a point, a line's or arc's points, or a text;
on a selected thing, move everything selected.
Dropping a point on a point merges them, and a line on a line merges those.
A click without moving selects the thing alone; with Shift, it adds the thing or takes it out.
At nothing, sweep a box: rightward it selects the lines, arcs, texts and lone points
wholly inside it, leftward those it touches; with Shift it adds to the selection.
A click at nothing clears the selection.
With an anchor, Shift and button 2 on a selected thing turns and sizes the selection about the anchor,
as the mouse goes around it and nearer or farther;
the turn takes a multiple of 15° within 2°.
On a Mac, button 2 is Option and button 1.
.TP
button 3
The menu:
.BR Cut ,
.BR Copy ,
.BR Paste ,
.BR Undo ,
.BR Write ,
.B Exit
and
.BR More… ,
which makes the next button 3 show every command, named as here but capitalized,
.BR Prune ,
which deletes the points no line or arc uses,
.BR Points ,
which shows or hides the points, and the
definitions, as
.BR "Copy par" .
Its commands act on what the mouse was at when the button went down;
after a command chosen there, the message line names its key.
.TP
wheel
Zoom around the mouse.
.PD
.PP
During a drag, Escape or another button takes back everything the drag did.
.SS Keys
.TF space
.TP
.BR Command-Z ", control-Z"
Undo.
.TP
.BR Command-Shift-Z ", Command-Y, control-Y, control-R"
Redo what undo took back.
.TP
.BR Command-C ", control-C"
Copy what is selected, or else what is here, to
.BR /dev/snarf
as a picture: the things, the points they stand on, and the constraints among them.
.TP
.BR Command-X ", control-X"
Copy, then delete.
.TP
.BR Command-V ", control-V"
Paste under the mouse, at the same size, and select what was pasted.
A snarf that isn't a picture becomes a text of its first line.
.TP
.BR Command-A ", control-A"
Select everything.
.TP
.BR Command-S ", control-S"
Write the file.
.TP
.BR Delete ", Backspace"
Delete what is selected, or else what is here,
with the points that only its lines and arcs used.
.TP
.B c
Make the point here, or a new one, the anchor, which shows as a blue ring.
The next button 1 draws an arc around it; after that the anchor stays, for
.BR r ,
.BR s ,
Shift and button 2, its guides, and
.BR o ,
until Escape lifts it.
.TP
.B t
Start a text here.
Type it; Backspace, control-U and control-W edit it, and newline, Escape or a click ends it.
.TP
.B r
Turn the piece here: what lines, arcs and constraints join to it,
or, if there are any, the things designated.
It turns around the anchor if there is one, else around the piece's one fixed point
if it has one, and else around the middle of the piece,
as the mouse goes around that point.
A line or text here snaps to a direction within 2° of a multiple of 15°.
Typing a number turns it that many degrees, Backspace edits the number,
and erasing it gives the turn back to the mouse;
the wheel turns it 3°, and the mouse no longer snaps after that.
.B s
changes to sizing.
A click or newline keeps it;
Escape or another button takes it back.
What it turns is held where the turn puts it, as a drag holds what it moves,
and the rest of the drawing meets the constraints around it,
so turning a linkage's crank moves the linkage,
in steps of 2° at most however far the mouse or a typed number jumps;
when it is let go, all the constraints are met again,
so a line held horizontal turns back, and fixed points stay fixed where the turn put them.
.TP
.B s
Size the piece here, as
.B r
turns it: by the mouse's distance from the point, by a number typed, or by the wheel.
Lengths held by
.B D
scalars and by
.B W
and
.B Y
size with it.
.B r
changes to turning.
.TP
.B x
Erase what is here, and what depends on it.
.TP
.B h
Make the line here horizontal or vertical, whichever is nearer.
.TP
.B f
Fix what is here so the solver leaves it alone, or free it.
Moving a thing frees it.
.TP
.B "p = m"
Copy the definitions
.BR par ,
.B eq
and
.B mid
(see Definitions): parallel or perpendicular, equal length, a midpoint.
.TP
.B a
Make what is here an attacher of this drawing, or not.
Only points, lines, arcs and texts attach.
.TP
.B d
Select, or designate, what is here for the next copy, or for
.B r
and
.BR s .
Designated things show in blue with their order;
.B d
on one moves it last, and on the last one forgets it.
.B d
at nothing starts a box: sweep it with button 1.
Swept rightward, it designates the lines, arcs, texts and lone points wholly inside it;
leftward, those it touches.
Undo, and Escape during a copy, keep the designations and the anchor.
.TP
.B e
Make the point here hot, so it has guides in every drag, or not.
Hot points show as gray squares.
.TP
.B o
On a line, make circles of its length, or stop; at a point, with an anchor, circles of the
distance between them; elsewhere, type a radius in inches and newline
to make circles of it, or stop, or newline alone to stop all.
A length typed or measured becomes a fixed scalar of the drawing.
The lengths, and the slopes, are saved in the file, as
.B "# sk lengths"
and
.BR "# sk slopes" ,
and undo takes them back.
.TP
.B /
On a line, make guides at its slope, or stop; elsewhere, type a slope in degrees from level and
newline to make guides at it, or stop, or newline alone to go back to level and upright.
.TP
.B n
Drop the constraints the last command, such as
.B h
or a copy, made, keeping where things went.
.TP
.B "g < >"
Gravity off or on, half or twice as far, here or during a drag.
.TP
.B k
Show or hide the constraints that hold.
.TP
.B space
Solve.
.TP
.B u
Undo; it keeps the last hundred changes.
.TP
.B .
Center the view here.
.TP
.B w
Write the file.
.TP
.B q
Exit; with changes not written, the first says so and the second exits.
.TP
Escape
Lift the anchor, and forget the designations and the hot points.
.PD
.SS Constraints
Each constraint shows as a circle with its letter, Sketchpad's, with arms to the points it
holds:
.B L
three points in a line,
.B C
two points as far from a center,
.B H
horizontal or vertical,
.B P
parallel or perpendicular,
.B M
lengths in a ratio,
.B X
a midpoint,
.B D
a scalar that is a distance, and
.B W
and
.B Y
for linkages.
A constraint that doesn't hold always shows, in red.
.B x
on a shown mark deletes it.
.SS Definitions
A definition is a drawing with attachers, in
.B /lib/sketch
or
.BR $home/lib/sketch .
Copying one puts a copy in the drawing:
its next-to-last attacher merges with what the mark is at,
its last follows the mouse, and a click drops it, merging it with what it lands on,
and only then are the copy's constraints met;
earlier attachers merge with the things designated, in order.
The menu lists every definition.
.SS Atoms
An atom is a line of words, quoted as in
.IR rc (1);
a line that begins
.B #
is a comment.
.TF "menu item"
.TP
.BI m " buttons point name"
The mouse, with these buttons down, at this point on the page, in inches as
.IB x , y\f1,
and at the thing of this name.
Either the point or the name may be left out.
If the thing named is there and the point is not at it, the name wins:
the mouse goes to the point or text named,
or onto the line or arc, at its middle if no point is given.
.TP
.BI k " key"
A key: the character, or
.BI 0x hex
for its rune.
.TP
.BI menu " item"
The item chosen from button 3's menu, or none.
.TP
.BI snarf " n"
The
.I n
bytes on the next lines are what a paste read from
.BR /dev/snarf .
.TP
.BI scale " s\fR, \fPdpi " n
The zoom, in pixels an inch, and the display's pixels an inch, which set how far the mark reaches.
.PD
.PP
The log begins with
.B scale
and
.BR dpi ,
names each thing it points at as the drawing will, and gives every move of a drag.
.SS Sketch
.I Sketch
is
.I sketch
in an
.IR acme (1)
window, whose body is the drawing: the text under it is the picture's
.IR pic (1),
rewritten after each change, so
.B Put
saves it.
With no
.IR file ,
.I Sketch
draws the window it is run from,
.BR $winid ;
with one, outside a window, or in a window whose
.B event
another program holds through
.IR amux (4),
as
.I win
does, it opens a new window,
with the file's text if it exists.
.PP
.I Sketch
adds itself to the window's tag.
.B Sketch
there ends it, and the window shows its text, to edit as any other;
.B Sketch
again draws it, or says in
.B +Errors
why it cannot.
While the drawing shows,
.B Undo
and
.B Redo
are
.IR sketch 's,
and a command beginning with
.BR | ,
.B <
or
.B >
pipes the selection as
.IR acme (1)
does a selection of text, but as its
.I pic
text, running the command in the file's directory:
what comes back replaces the selection where it was,
keeping its names and the points it shares with the rest of the drawing.
The menu's commands are
.IR Sketch 's
in the tag too, acting on what the mouse was last at in the drawing.
.B Del
deletes the window, ending
.I Sketch
with it.
.PP
.I Rot
and
.I scale
are such commands: they turn a picture's points about their middle,
90° if not told, and scale them, twice if not told.
.I Len
holds each line of a picture at
.IR length ,
or at the length it has if not told, by adding a fixed scalar and a
.B D
constraint for it.
